#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114989 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "239ee94c-c3d9-5955-8cdd-afff77ec3279" :question/math-academy-id "e-4716" :question/problem "Is \n$\\begin{aligned}E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$ \nan elementary matrix?" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation.\n\nThe given matrix $E$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I_2$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_2:= R_2+(-5) R_1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114990 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c56b3e9-cf23-5e1e-8c7d-c474059c9a9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "40cdd39f-29c3-5dad-9708-02c3e0de6acb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4718" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/65e27c97ac04ff5514c6e1be3b010e7a6efa464131bfbad03a569f85e728ac85.png)" :question/worked-solution "We write down $E$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/cd8839007e548fcd0858ebc42904d3659d639da834b8caa476c4765b5b1a6b69.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114991 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd12bc7b-1b5d-5af2-bcef-c9d0fda7d8fc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Product Involving the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "ed71f469-f0e9-5016-846d-cb70d59ab3ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-4719" :question/problem "Find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1},$ where ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/ad2295d047cfcd60a05c2b155e8c21c511d295e76b0ad4056cf136fe54c272a1.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/299ce95f91a5eb4029b2a8512c0b45c24562fac91cc4c70a7e00ecf42c320099.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $E_1$ and $E_2$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find $E_2^{-1}.$ We write down $E_2$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/a8c0d76a5f06d1077eb45b6af4761434164602747f54d9644646b654d319b373.png)\n\nNow, we can find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/f16a0b0e573cf3a8022f9c62993364a0d708c3a0cff0b4bb4cb0eadde7848f56.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9be94925-76c1-59da-ab5d-42b8f113735e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335561} {:answer/id #uuid "dd3baef6-689b-5d31-8dbd-3b7e9b96787a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335562} {:answer/id #uuid "6726dbee-06e3-569f-8059-970a58929e8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335563} {:answer/id #uuid "999fc489-c752-5a26-8d5d-b2045af01258" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335564} {:answer/id #uuid "379e8629-2c01-5b9f-95b0-4803d84b2fdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9be94925-76c1-59da-ab5d-42b8f113735e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335561} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a22e2fd-275d-5913-9b1b-d800cc087462" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d49dd3c-2d26-5daf-baa6-13e98e158d10" :question/math-academy-id "q-130772" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrices $P$ and $Q$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $S$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =S\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $S$ is an elementary matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3b5f78f-1e63-5800-ae3e-5d8f9a18d9bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335568} {:answer/id #uuid "14c2de26-a3a9-5bf4-b032-07d4f0db7642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335569} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d8bddf-f629-504e-aa49-6e2b13c5c5f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335570} {:answer/id #uuid "3dc2ff11-20a9-50aa-bf2b-98fa5d5b517f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335571} {:answer/id #uuid "8968f30f-7a08-5194-b917-09339fb2dc5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0d8bddf-f629-504e-aa49-6e2b13c5c5f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335570} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ddb4f9c-b793-51c0-9fde-49a175491b33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17df8f70-560c-50cc-995c-b38203b5ea8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-130774" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ are elementary matrices."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5a8d076-21a2-59eb-8e3f-f57d1fb5c3a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335575} {:answer/id #uuid "05a6a4bc-86d5-5b80-98f4-d19e3099ae51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335576} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c11f18-f9f6-562f-8d27-b02995ffbac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$  and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335577} {:answer/id #uuid "e13f934e-1975-5d9d-b6af-ff11e1a58dd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335578} {:answer/id #uuid "0b5da5f7-8432-5cf7-9b6e-3820a1e1265b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$  and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e13f934e-1975-5d9d-b6af-ff11e1a58dd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335578} :answer-field/id #uuid "c89188d7-c542-5183-ad0d-be2390599e11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea3b1565-0857-5d0a-8afe-805754b2cf2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-130778" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrices $A$ and $C$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=4{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:=4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is an elementary matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c56b3e9-cf23-5e1e-8c7d-c474059c9a9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15113449-19ab-50b0-8134-3ab706171138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335582} {:answer/id #uuid "3381ad05-9cbc-5548-b98b-dada96cd623e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335583} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd431f0-01a2-5e74-a3c0-4426075eb325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335584} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb900b4-7e35-5302-895e-26bacac22914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335585} {:answer/id #uuid "5dfe3519-92e3-55d6-8204-d3effdc43819" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3381ad05-9cbc-5548-b98b-dada96cd623e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335583} :answer-field/id #uuid "a31b77a4-d2ec-595c-8ed2-3296ead9f7ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0ecc21c-a347-5c4c-ab45-0272be6860db" :question/math-academy-id "q-130781" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $B$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 10 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c56b3e9-cf23-5e1e-8c7d-c474059c9a9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7244880-0e53-5ad2-8b66-c5a9b6c05122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335589} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe02594-6a21-5e79-9407-caf00c1220fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335590} {:answer/id #uuid "2cc9a355-46d6-5b57-8384-b24c8740554b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335591} {:answer/id #uuid "be14afdf-8ac2-52d9-86eb-94fa20fc7e50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335592} {:answer/id #uuid "15810dea-da40-51d8-aac1-2dec6aefc072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7244880-0e53-5ad2-8b66-c5a9b6c05122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335589} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cca892b-09d2-51f0-ab58-321e006046dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "211a8774-809f-5621-9d39-cfa06f2d1523" :question/math-academy-id "q-130796" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c56b3e9-cf23-5e1e-8c7d-c474059c9a9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0baf4f30-143a-5734-8e82-d74b1455923c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335596} {:answer/id #uuid "1c6ce4e5-9933-5764-a310-b7b6a62bb82a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{7} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335597} {:answer/id #uuid "b7352a55-bf67-