uestions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd12bc7b-1b5d-5af2-bcef-c9d0fda7d8fc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Product Involving the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "171f43a1-dc71-56dd-882a-0ac6bd227973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 24 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335603} {:answer/id #uuid "a887798b-9e33-51e5-805f-7ad39ec536ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 24 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335604} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd1e573-3711-5329-a5af-9cc7a39fece8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335605} {:answer/id #uuid "161a1904-f175-5fd5-8c4e-5ee02c38e3f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335606} {:answer/id #uuid "8dde1d06-4481-574c-a4d0-6eae770ef162" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cd1e573-3711-5329-a5af-9cc7a39fece8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335605} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cb4f32b-e128-5a58-8149-720d77a8c91f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e20cc98-116e-57df-aec7-6d2c0f8b7480" :question/math-academy-id "q-130812" :question/problem "Find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd12bc7b-1b5d-5af2-bcef-c9d0fda7d8fc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Product Involving the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c47cca2-7494-512c-8ba1-fe029563f8a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335610} {:answer/id #uuid "8480bc6f-6082-5e03-a1b4-9cb476a1396f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}21 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335611} {:answer/id #uuid "374c299d-e14e-5258-aad7-7283760b0996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335612} {:answer/id #uuid "eb294f2a-a7db-596c-b3f1-dcc01f9ccf1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}19 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335613} {:answer/id #uuid "d317860b-64e4-575a-bf44-81dff7155ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 & -7 \\\\ 8 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8480bc6f-6082-5e03-a1b4-9cb476a1396f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}21 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335611} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2d450ce-fa90-5a5c-a8c4-3e5061d61e8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac2e42f8-d970-5a58-b537-a5e42b74e334" :question/math-academy-id "q-130823" :question/problem "Find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}21 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78b25b29-9f4b-55f4-8d20-7dd5a3264cdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ and $Y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335617} {:answer/id #uuid "a22b2205-2e1d-5a19-8d50-600de488fd54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Y$ and $Z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335618} {:answer/id #uuid "5155d5c1-8f40-50c6-9286-a0253a031e03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ and $Z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335619} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9ef7da-1104-5abb-a6bb-0bbe6479ead8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335620} {:answer/id #uuid "d7288cfc-53d4-5300-aa7a-d3d14db7e573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5155d5c1-8f40-50c6-9286-a0253a031e03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ and $Z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335619} :answer-field/id #uuid "acd03380-15f8-5494-9bfb-1dd9076f7879" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2181ca9-38bc-5236-abd5-9faccb5c3bb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-31968" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nW=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Z=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $W$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =W\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $Y$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $Z$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $W$ and $Z$ are elementary matrices."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "099779d5-9305-5bf2-87c8-a56aaf5ab415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335624} {:answer/id #uuid "47994b21-fd82-54ca-a5c3-329d090edb94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$E$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335625} {:answer/id #uuid "42f6c261-4717-5cae-a78c-325aed2aa77c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335626} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0564f8-c169-5ed9-a7ed-388dfe1ae943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ and $E$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335627} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb79d30-f760-5444-ad9f-91e29a499a03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $E$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bb79d30-f760-5444-ad9f-91e29a499a03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $E$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335628} :answer-field/id #uuid "33c63b95-1ef2-5d16-8e52-abe856b9942c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "848eb5be-a7d1-5be6-a75a-963b36a832db" :question/math-academy-id "q-42751" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $Q$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=3{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =Q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $W$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $E$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-4){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $Q$ and $E$ are elementary matrices."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335629 :knowledge-point/_questions [